<p>France is sending a major naval force toward the Middle East, with President Emmanuel Macron casting the move as both protection for allies and a possible contribution to escorting commercial ships through the Strait of Hormuz amid the Iran war. Macron said he expects the conflict to last “several days, maybe several weeks,” as European leaders weigh how to prevent the fighting from spreading beyond the immediate combat zone. The announcement came as European officials closed ranks around Cyprus after the EU member was hit by what Macron called attacks “from multiple drones and missiles,” including a strike on a British base on the island. Macron, joined by Cypriot President Nikos Christodoulides and Greek Prime Minister Kyriakos Mitsotakis, argued that an attack on Cyprus amounts to an attack on Europe—language echoed in coverage of an emergency trilateral meeting in Paphos aimed at coordinating regional security. Macron also said France and its partners are preparing a “purely defensive” escort mission to help gradually reopen the Strait of Hormuz once the most intense phase of fighting has passed, pointing to the waterway’s outsized role in global energy and trade flows.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/gelXEKwsUEkeFsEKoghe0iHz2LUzOZJcqVsF9A2_xmCQsbXXVAvhe3elZG-Nq4WjLfczIHd1kSnxLx_SjcOji2uCJklN-DZPvzQ_HFi_upjH' alt='French President Emmanuel Macron visits the bridge of the aircraft carrier Charles de Gaulle after Iranian drone strikes on Cyprus, March 9, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Carrier visit detail: Macron flew by helicopter to the French aircraft carrier Charles de Gaulle during its deployment to the Mediterranean.</li><li>Coalition makeup: Macron said European and non-European states could join the proposed defensive effort.</li><li>Conflict phase: Macron said the Middle East war has entered its second week.</li><li>Cyprus framing: Macron described Cyprus as the first European state to be directly affected by the Iran war.</li></ul><